--TEST--
Core\Jwt::signObject refuses a claims object with no JSON encoding and one carrying either claim the member writes itself, each as a bug in the program rather than a verdict on a token — `rule:security/jwt-expiry-is-mandatory`, `rule:security/protocol-roster`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The edges of what `object` lets through. The parameter's type is what refuses
// an `int` or a `string` here, so every refusal left is about an object that is
// structured and still not claims: one no encoder can write, and one that has
// already written a claim this member owns. Both are `LogicError` — the program
// built the value, and nothing about a token is wrong.
final class Plain {
    public string $sub;

    public function constructor(string $sub) {
        $this->sub = $sub;
    }
}

Core\Crypto\KeyPair $pair = Core\Crypto::generateKeyPair(Core\Crypto\KeyKind::P256);

// A class joins a wire format by carrying the attribute, and this one does not.
try {
    Core\Jwt::signObject(new Plain("ada"), 15m, $pair);
    echo "a class with no codec signed a token", "\n";
} catch (LogicError $noCodec) {
    echo $noCodec->message, "\n";
}

// `exp` is not the caller's to set — `rule:security/jwt-expiry-is-mandatory` has
// no flag and no field either, so a claims object naming it is refused rather
// than overwritten. `iat` goes the same way for the same reason: the member
// writes both, and a payload carrying two of a name is not a document.
try {
    Core\Jwt::signObject(
        Core\Arr::shapeAs<{sub: string, exp: int}>(["sub" => "ada", "exp" => 4]),
        15m,
        $pair
    );
    echo "a claims object set its own expiry", "\n";
} catch (LogicError $ownExpiry) {
    echo $ownExpiry->message, "\n";
}

try {
    Core\Jwt::signObject(
        Core\Arr::shapeAs<{iat: int, sub: string}>(["iat" => 0, "sub" => "ada"]),
        15m,
        $pair
    );
    echo "a claims object set its own issued-at", "\n";
} catch (LogicError $ownIssuedAt) {
    echo $ownIssuedAt->message, "\n";
}

// And the lifetime is bounded here exactly as it is on `sign`: one member, one
// answer, because both read it through the same function.
try {
    Core\Jwt::signObject(Core\Arr::shapeAs<{sub: string}>(["sub" => "ada"]), 500ms, $pair);
    echo "a token shorter than a second was signed", "\n";
} catch (LogicError $tooShort) {
    echo $tooShort->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
Core\Jwt::signObject(): $claims is written as `Core\Json::encode` writes it, and an instance of `Plain` has no JSON encoding — a class participates by carrying `#[Json\Derive]`.
Core\Jwt::signObject(): $claims carries a field named `exp`, which this member writes from $lifetime — the clock is not the caller's to set, so that a token this member produces always carries an expiry it will accept.
Core\Jwt::signObject(): $claims carries a field named `iat`, which this member writes from $lifetime — the clock is not the caller's to set, so that a token this member produces always carries an expiry it will accept.
Core\Jwt::signObject(): $lifetime is 500000000ns, and a token has to be valid for at least one whole second — `exp` counts seconds (RFC 7519 § 4.1.4), so anything shorter signs a token that is already past it.
